Consider the following modal language MT interpreted over metric spaces. Fix a countably infinite set of propositional variables. The language MT is built from these variables using:
- the Boolean connectives ¬ and ∧;
- for each positive rational a, the unary "metric" modalities ∃^{<a} φ and ∃^{≤a} φ;
- the topological interior operator I and closure operator C (induced by the metric topology);
- the universal modality ∀ and the existential modality ∃.

Models are pairs (X, V), where (X, d) is a metric space and V assigns to each propositional variable a subset of X. Truth at a point x ∈ X is defined inductively as usual, with:
- (X, V), x ⊨ ∃^{<a} φ iff there exists y ∈ X with d(x, y) < a and (X, V), y ⊨ φ;
- (X, V), x ⊨ ∃^{≤a} φ iff there exists y ∈ X with d(x, y) ≤ a and (X, V), y ⊨ φ;
- (X, V), x ⊨ Iφ iff there exists ε > 0 such that for all y with d(x,y) < ε, (X, V), y ⊨ φ;
- (X, V), x ⊨ Cφ iff for every ε > 0 there exists y with d(x,y) < ε and (X, V), y ⊨ φ;
- (X, V), x ⊨ ∀φ (resp. ∃φ) iff φ holds at every (resp. some) point of X.

Let 𝒞 denote the class of metric spaces (X, d) whose induced topological space is connected.

(a) Provide a sound and complete recursive axiomatisation of the set of MT-formulas valid in every model based on a space in 𝒞.

(b) Determine the computational complexity (and in particular decidability) of the satisfiability problem for MT-formulas over models based on spaces in 𝒞.

